    <channel>
        <title>DuoUpdater</title>
        <!--
          The Sparkle feed. `scripts/publish-release.sh` regenerates this file on
          every release and pushes it here. The app reads it through
          https://updates.duoupdater.app/appcast.xml (workers/appcast relays this
          file); builds before that read
          https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jizhi0v0/duo-updater/main/appcast.xml
          directly, so this path must keep working.

          Deliberately empty rather than absent: an appcast with no items means
          "nothing newer available", which Sparkle handles as up-to-date, whereas
          a missing file is a fetch error on every check. The first release fills
          it in.

          Release history starts here. Earlier builds were published from a
          separate binary-only repo that existed only while the source was
          private, and are not carried over.
        -->
        <item>
            <title>0.5.17</title>
            <pubDate>Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:42:02 +0800</pubDate>
            <link>https://github.com/jizhi0v0/duo-updater/releases/tag/v0.5.17</link>
            <sparkle:version>135</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.5.17</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>15.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description sparkle:format="markdown"><![CDATA[**The apps list now sorts by what changed most recently.** A Sort menu in the window's toolbar and in the popover switches between Name and Recent. Recent uses the release date the vendor itself states, so an update published this week comes before one from last month; an app whose source states no date stays in name order.

**DSH NEXT is supported beside DSH Desktop.** The pre-release line of the community DSH Desktop client installs as its own app, so both tracks can sit side by side and each one is updated from its own releases.

**DeepSeek Harness updates in one click.** Its official feed describes the download in a layout DuoUpdater used to misread, so it could say a new version existed but left you to fetch and install it. It now downloads, verifies and installs it like any other app.

**uv and mise tools are found even when uv or mise itself came from Homebrew.** The heading above a group of tools is also a compact summary now; the full paths are one click away, ready to copy.
]]></description>
